Market Drivers
Expansion of EV Charging Infrastructure — Rising EV adoption and charging-point additions are increasing demand for power-conversion modules.
Shift Toward Fast and Ultra-Fast Charging — Higher-power DC chargers require multiple power modules, increasing module content per charging site.
800V Architectures and SiC Technology — Higher-voltage EV platforms and silicon-carbide semiconductors are supporting greater efficiency and power density.

Vertical Industry Watch
Power modules are becoming a critical component of the rapidly expanding DC charging infrastructure. Modern charging cabinets can combine multiple standardized modules to achieve higher power levels while maintaining serviceability and flexible system architecture. The 35–50 kW class is an important building block for contemporary fast-charging systems, while higher-power charging increasingly requires multiple modules operating together. SiC-based designs are gaining attention because higher switching frequencies and lower losses can improve efficiency and reduce cooling requirements. Thermal design is also becoming a major product differentiator, with suppliers developing forced-air and liquid-cooled configurations for different operating environments. Software diagnostics, remote monitoring, V2G functionality, and grid-interaction capabilities are further expanding the role of power modules.

